A||||9||Steve Normington||OBD II Engine Help||||4||
Z||000000||Steve Normington||07-20-2001||04:26 PM||||I have a 3.4L engine/wiring/computer out of a 97 Olds Silhoutte in my Fiero (SFI, DIS). I also have the OBD II laptop hookup from <A HREF="http://www.OBD-2.com" TARGET=_blank>www.OBD-2.com</A>  installed. I am using a manual transmission (the Olds was AT only).<P>The car will start and then immediantly die. Occasionally it will after fire when it dies. After the engine stops, I hear a whining sound for about 2-3 sec (not the fuel pump). I think this may be the EGR valve (electric) closing. The fuel pressure is 42-45 psi during the entire time. The OBD II returns an error that the fan relay and the canister purge solenoid are not hooked up (which they aren't). Also, some of the sensors (coolant level, oil level, transmission fluid temperature, transmission shift) are not connected. The computer indicates that the transmission is in gear 3. The 24x crankshaft sensor is connected, but the trigger wheel has been removed so no signal is being sent to the computer. I had the ignition module tested at NAPA last week and they said that it is good. The OBD II indicates that the A/F ratio is 7:1 (the engine does seem to be rich) and the spark advance is -20 Deg when the engine fires up. The computer seems to be getting the proper tach signal (200-300 rpm cranking, 1100-1200 rpm when it starts).<P>Could the canister purge cause the engine to die?<BR>Could the limp-home mode prevent the car from running?<BR>What could cause the A/F to be so rich (open loop)?<P>theorge, I think I have given all pertinant data.||||reg||4
Z||000001||00lE||07-20-2001||04:47 PM||||Without both crank signals I doubt that it will run. The sensor directly over the timing chain determines when to fire the injectors on a 3100/3400 SFI motor. The sensor on the side above the oil pan is for spark timing.  OBD II sucks anyway, I would go with a MPFI PCM like the 1227730 or 1227727.  Just a suggestion since thats what I did on my 3100 (7727) and its runs just fine..<BR>You should also verify that your fuel pump runs after the initial fire.  The key on prime will allow it to start, but without the pump running it will stop almost immediatly.  You should be able to jumper the fuel pump to run continuous at the solenoid (run 12v) for testing purposes.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 07-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Joe Torma||07-20-2001||05:03 PM|||| Steve,<BR>Anything is possible with the OBD II.  You HAVE to have ALL of the timing triggers hooked up and functional!!!  2 off the crank and 1 off of the cam.  I don't think the computer knows the engine started at all.<P>The Service manual says the 24x trigger will cause the engine to misfire/cut out.<P>Make sure you did something with the theft deterrant signal Pin 55(Dk.Blue)(since you have fuel pressure it may kill the computer anyway)<P>Interesting...the computer should default to 4th gear.<P>Check your sensor feeds...like pin 18(red/wht)<P>I think the buzz/whir is the Idle Air trying to open up.<P>Hey...what did you do with the a/c compressor?<P>If you blow up your PCM you can have mine...was going to sacrifice it to the OBD gods, but pay for shipping and it's yours!  I also have some other parts you may be interested in.<P>Joe<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/3400.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Steve Normington||07-20-2001||05:29 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 00lE:<BR><B>Without both crank signals I doubt that it will run. The sensor directly over the timing chain determines when to fire the injectors on a 3100/3400 SFI motor. The sensor on the side above the oil pan is for spark timing.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The one that is missing is the one mounted on the front of the timing cover with the trigger wheel on the harmonic balancer. The cam and crank in the side of the block are connected and seem to be working fine.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>OBD II sucks anyway, I would go with a MPFI PCM like the 1227730 or 1227727.  Just a suggestion since thats what I did on my 3100 (7727) and its runs just fine.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I would do that, but I an NOT rewiring this car anymore. I've already done 3 sets of wiring and I'm done.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>You should also verify that your fuel pump runs after the initial fire.  The key on prime will allow it to start, but without the pump running it will stop almost immediatly.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The fuel pump seems to be fine. The fuel manifold pressure never drops below 42 psi.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B>Make sure you did something with the theft deterrant signal Pin 55(Dk.Blue)(since you have fuel pressure it may kill the computer anyway)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What do I do with it, short to ground, short to power, dance around it with a holy gourd/sandal?<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>Check your sensor feeds...like pin 18(red/wht)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Feed to cam/crank sensor? Should this be 5 or 12 V?<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>Hey...what did you do with the a/c compressor?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's hanging by a chain from the r/h hood hinge until I weld up a bracket for it.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>If you blow up your PCM you can have mine...was going to sacrifice it to the OBD gods, but pay for shipping and it's yours! I also have some other parts you may be interested in.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE></B><P>Is it a PCM that will run a 3.4 L MT in DSI mode without a 24x crank? Email me a list of the parts, please.<p>[This message has been edited by Steve Normington (edited 07-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Joe Torma||07-20-2001||05:41 PM||||The dance might help...I'll look the circuit up for you.  Get that front trigger to work again...I bet that is your main problem.<P>I have the exact same motor as you...out of the Olds.<P>I'll email you the parts I have.<P>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000005||00lE||07-20-2001||06:06 PM||||      <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR>[B<BR>Is it a PCM that will run a 3.4 L MT in DSI mode without a 24x crank? <BR>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OBD I PCM's like the 1227730 or 1227727 use only one crank sensor (side of block).  You can also customize the code to your engine/mods by burning a chip (vs. flash reprogramming an OBD II which is quite an expensive endeavor).  And, if you get tired of the 3400 after a while, you can drop in a TPI 350 V8, change out the calpack and be good to go.  Then of course there is the fact that the only turbocharged 60 degree V6 made by GM used the 1227727... I could go on.<P>The reason I suggested that you verify the fuel pump operation is that newer PCM's monitor the fuel pump voltage, not the pressure.  If there is no voltage to the fuel pump after initial crank, the PCM shuts the motor down immediatly to prevent damage due to a lean burn condition.<BR>An incorrctly wired fuel pump circuit or a defectvie oil pressure switch (if used) will cause the exact condition I described.  The easiest way to remove this factor is to jumper the fuel pump at the relay.  Just a thought..<P><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 07-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||07-20-2001||07:39 PM||||I got some advice from Chris West that got me running with my '97 swap;put large resistors in the circuit that normally has shifter solenoids(they get hot so don't include them in the harness wrap),do something to attenuate the theft circuit(I purchased a small,cheater circuit from Chris as well,it's potted in silicone so I can't tell you what it is exactly),and I believe that you'll need to hook up the speedo(vss)wires that should be the same color as original if you haven't done that.<BR>This is the info I got in exchange for some $400 in clutch parts that I've yet to recieve any compensation for,and I just told you for free!<BR>The fuel mixture tuning thing is another story,I fixed it with an adjustable controller($400) wired piggy-back onto the airflow meter circuit.If you can use the early PCM,you're better off until the software for changing this stuff is a little more popular/cheaper.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Steve Normington||07-20-2001||10:56 PM||||OOIE and lowCG, thanks for the info. I may switch to the earlier ECM's to avoid all of this excrement.<P>Note to OOIE: What's with the handle?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||07-20-2001||10:58 PM||||If that PCM is coded for passkey, there's no way it'll start without feeding it the proper signal. It turns off the injector outputs if it's not there. It's looking for a ~50hz square wave at the blue wire. You can build a circuit to bypass it for about $3 worth of Radio Shack parts. <BR>I think you will also need to do something with all those other open inputs if you want it to run properly.<P>I agree with 00lE about switching to a pre-OBDII PCM. That seems to be the way to go for non-stock applications.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||00lE||07-20-2001||11:21 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B>Note to OOIE: What's with the handle?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you install a 3100 SFI engine in a Fiero, it is now known as a 001E.  Or at least thats what is says on the intake plenum.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 07-20-2001).]||||reg||1
